---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/consent';
const title = 'SMS Consent for Marketing Campaigns | Shopify SMS Marketing';
const desc = 'Keep a usable record of channel permission from capture through opt-out and migration. A phone number or an email subscription alone does not establish SMS eligibility.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/guides" class="link text-sm">← All SMS guides</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Trace permission through the campaign system</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">SMS Consent for Marketing Campaigns</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">{desc}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify">
<h2>Separate identity from permission</h2>
<p>A checkout phone number can identify a customer without making that person an SMS marketing subscriber. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/sms/deliverability">Shopify explicitly distinguishes email consent from SMS consent</a> and advises clear SMS opt-in and expectations about message type and frequency. Checked November 25, 2026. Segment membership, past purchases and high lifetime spend do not replace that permission.</p>
<p>This is an operational recordkeeping workflow. Determine the requirements for your markets, message categories and sending method before approving capture copy or a campaign. Do not treat a provider's default quiet-hours setting, a checkout checkbox or the checklist below as a complete legal determination.</p>
<h2>Keep the record behind the subscription flag</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Why the campaign team needs it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Customer and destination</td><td>Identify the relevant phone number, store and channel without merging unrelated permissions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capture event</td><td>Source, timestamp with timezone, form or keyword identifier and method</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disclosure version</td><td>Exact copy and presentation shown at that time, including linked terms where relevant</td></tr>
<tr><td>State and history</td><td>Pending, subscribed, unsubscribed or unknown, plus later changes and their source</td></tr>
<tr><td>Campaign scope</td><td>Message category and expectations associated with that capture</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>These fields are a proposed audit trail, not a claim that every jurisdiction mandates this exact schema. Retain original evidence with suitable access controls and a retention policy. If an old record lacks evidence, label the gap; do not manufacture a timestamp or disclosure version from today's form.</p>
<h2>Map four capture paths</h2>
<p>For a checkout checkbox, preserve the form version and the action that changed SMS status. For a popup, test mobile presentation and whether the checkbox or submit action is clear. For keyword capture, retain the keyword context and confirmation path. For a QR code, inspect the destination page actually shown on a phone; scanning a code is not by itself a campaign subscription record.</p>
<p>Build controlled profiles for SMS-only, email-only, both-channel and unknown-permission states. Confirm each enters only its intended marketing audience. Include any confirmation step your chosen capture flow uses; a pending profile must not silently become subscribed because an import mapped an empty field incorrectly. These are proposed checks, not reported test results.</p>
<h2>Make revocation reach the queue</h2>
<p>Document how keyword replies, preference pages and support requests change permission. Assign an owner to requests that arrive outside the automated path. Test an opt-out while a campaign is queued and while a delayed flow is waiting. Check the sending system's decision at dispatch as well as the customer profile shown in the store.</p>
<p>Do not infer a universal cross-channel effect from one opt-out field. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/customers/import-export-customers">Shopify's customer CSV documentation</a> says an email opt-out does not remove SMS or WhatsApp consent. Interpret the customer's actual request, applicable requirements and provider behavior; preserve separate channel states and any broader request. Avoid sending a marketing message simply to ask an opted-out person to opt back in.</p>
<h2>Migrate evidence, not just a yes/no column</h2>
<ol><li>Export the available permission history, capture evidence and suppression records before retiring the old sender.</li><li>Map each destination field explicitly. Keep unknown and unsubscribed records out of a subscribed import.</li><li>Reconcile counts by state and channel, then inspect sample identities with different histories.</li><li>Account for opt-outs occurring after the export through a final update or controlled cutover.</li><li>Disable duplicate sending paths and verify the new system's queued-message handling.</li></ol>
<p>A successful CSV upload proves the file was accepted, not that evidence or suppression survived. Record rejected rows, normalized phone numbers, merged identities and the reason each state changed. If the destination cannot preserve the original evidence, retain an accessible reference outside the sending tool rather than replacing it with a fabricated new opt-in.</p>
<h2>Release a campaign from a permission snapshot</h2>
<p>Before scheduling, record the audience definition, capture scope, exclusions, applicable timing policy and responsible approver. Recheck eligibility near dispatch where the tool supports it. A historical export can become stale after a new opt-out. If a connector fails, pause affected sends rather than assuming the last successful subscription snapshot is current.</p>
<p>When a customer changes a phone number, verify how the destination and permission history are associated. Do not automatically transfer consent between unrelated numbers or profiles. Give support staff a way to flag ambiguous cases without manually forcing the profile into a subscribed state.</p>
<p>Use <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> to carry exclusions between senders and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> to match the calendar to subscriber expectations. The output of this workflow is a traceable eligibility decision, not a vendor ranking or a promised conversion improvement.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
